=== Textstrategy end-to-end story log ===
Seed: 1 | Target day: 20 | Started: 2026-11-10T13:30:38.252Z


The sun is low in the west, and the sea beneath it turns from bronze to iron. The wind is warm and salt, with no rain in it yet. The light is going, but slowly; the evening will be long. This is the last boat. She is old and salt-blanched, and she carries more than she was built to carry: refugees and everything they own, bundles and children and cooking pots lashed down between the benches. The storm took the sail days ago, and the sea kept it. Since then everyone rows in turns, and the oars keep their slow rhythm on the water, hour upon hour, patient beyond the patience of any crew. Everyone is tired. The fresh water is nearly gone; there is only what the rains give, and the rains have been few. The food from home is long gone too: the bread went first, weeks ago, then everything else that could keep, and for a long time now everyone has lived on what the fishing lines bring up, raw fish and rain water, the catch of one day against the hunger of the next. There is no land in sight, in any direction. There is no way back. Among the rowers are your three friends: proud and determined Arik, glum and cynical Bearn, and clever Eya. Near you, your fourth friend Agnar sleeps under a tarp, worn hollow by the voyage. You decide not to wake him. Let the sleeping sleep. Your own turn at the oars is over, and Arik's turn has ended too. He is exhausted, but too proud to show it, and keeps rowing, reluctant to hand over the oar. Behind you, the next rowers settle into their seats, and the strokes go on.
> Watch the darkening sea and sky

You stand at the side and watch the evening come on. The water is dark and immense, lifting the boat and setting it down again in long, unhurried swells. A long rope of weed drifts past, going where the sea goes. Far off, lightning flickers inside the clouds: silent, slow, a light from a storm that has not reached you yet. You search the horizon and find nothing. No sail, no shore, no bird. Only the sea, and the last of the evening, and the wind on your face carrying the smell of coming rain.
> Get some rest

You find a place among the bundles, pull a corner of sail cloth over you, and let the day go. Sleep comes slowly, and against your will, but it comes. You dream of the village, of doors, of a table with bread on it, and the dreams are uneasy, thin at the edges. Still, the body knows what it needs. Hours pass. The oars go on. The rain, when it starts, drums on the tarp above you, patient and small. Then the wind changes. It hardens, the boat leans, and a rope somewhere slaps the mast, again and again. You wake with your heart already standing, and the boat is not the boat it was.
> Go back to sleep

The storm arrives in full darkness, at last, and total. Rain hammers the deck. The waves take the boat and throw it, and the boat, old creature, takes the punishment. People shout. Somewhere a child is crying, and someone is singing to the child, and both sound equally desperate. Then a cry goes up from the front, one word that cuts through everything: "REEF!" The boat grinds over the rocks, and the hull tears open. People and belongings spill into the black water together, and the water does not care which is which. You go in among them, into the cold and the shouting and the arms. Arik has come up shouting, calling names and orders to whoever can hear, needing nobody yet. Bearn's head goes under and comes up empty, and the cursing that follows has no strength in it. Somewhere off in the dark Eya is laughing, wild and thin, a man spending more than she has; she has caught a plank, and both hands want it. The families are folding into each other, ring inside ring, and no stranger in this water will stop for your friends. The rest of your own are finding each other in the churn, or being found. The three of them lie in three directions, and the surf is widening every gap. Who do you grab and hold onto?
> Arik

You reach for Arik. He takes your arm with a grip that refuses everything the sea has to offer, and together you go under, and together you come up, mouths full of salt. He coughs, spits, shouts words the storm devours. Then he pulls, and you pull, and by hands and stubbornness you are both moving through the mountains of water toward a dark line that must be shore. It must be. Arik spends a breath on you instead of the shore. "You held," he shouts into the wind. "The rest is just swimming." It is not. But you both swim better for hearing it.
> Continue to the shore

You crawl out of the surf onto wet sand and fall down, breathing hard. The sand holds you, solid and unmoving, the first thing in weeks that has not moved beneath you. The sleep you caught before the storm is spent now, every hour of it, and you let the sand have what is left. Around you, others stumble out of the dark: mothers calling names into the roar, men dragging bundles, an old man on his knees with both hands pressed to the ground. One of the refugees lies moaning on the sand, badly hurt, and the others gather around. Agnar walks out of the surf unaided, rested, if anyone on this beach is, and steadier than most. Behind you, the wreck settles on the reef, groaning with every wave, refusing even now to finish. And the storm, having broken her, is bringing her in: every wave that lifts her sets her down a little nearer the shore, closer than she has any right to be. Close enough that someone down the beach has found the way out to her, a line of shallower water between the rocks, and figures are wading along it already, hauling their own belongings back through the dark, salvaging while the sea is still too busy to notice. Nothing of yours came ashore with you. Everything you have in the world is still on that boat.
> Let the night come

The rain goes on, softer now, the storm having spent itself on your arrival. You count the others, and the count comes out: everyone made it ashore. The injured one is being cared for. The wreck groans on the reef behind you, breaking a little more with every wave, but still holding together. There is no going back now, and nowhere else to go. On this beach, tonight, that is not a threat. It is a fact, and facts can be built on. The survivors huddle together on the dark sand in the lee of the dunes, shoulder against shoulder, sharing what warmth the living still carry. Nobody speaks. Agnar sleeps again inside the huddle. The night deepens. The first day ashore waits for morning.


